// Doom's WAD, a level of it and the map of the level in ent-lang
// (examples/doom: wad.ent, level.ent, video.ent, automap.ent), on a small
// WAD made for it (Inputs/doom/make_wad.py): a square room with a step
// across it. The file is read into a buffer by the module `files`, its
// lumps and the level are read out of that by fns that are given the
// buffer, the lines are entities, and the map is drawn into a buffer a
// pixel at a time, by a proc that is given it to change.
// RUN: rm -rf %t && mkdir -p %t
// RUN: %python %S/Inputs/doom/make_wad.py %t
// RUN: cd %t && %ent run -I %S/../../examples/doom \
// RUN:     %S/../../examples/doom/check/automap_check.ent | FileCheck %s
// The picture: its header, and as many bytes as 320 by 200 pixels take.
// RUN: head -c 14 %t/level.ppm | FileCheck %s --check-prefix=PICTURE
// RUN: wc -c < %t/level.ppm | FileCheck %s --check-prefix=SIZE

// CHECK:      wad 1370 bytes, 12 lumps; level lump 1
// CHECK-NEXT: vertexes 6 3875660850 sectors 2 2193965797 sides 7 737302980
// CHECK-NEXT: lines 8
// The room is as high as the map's part of the picture (168 pixels to
// 256 units); the line that is not to be drawn is not; the walls are
// 670 pixels and the step 169.
// CHECK-NEXT: map scale 43008 from 0 0: 839 pixels lit, 3924037125
// CHECK-NOT:  {{.}}

// PICTURE: P6 320 200 255
// SIZE: 192015
